---
import SiteLayout from "@/layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import swarmStart from "../../../../docs/desktop/assets/electron/swarms/swarm-start-large.png";
import swarmWatch from "../../../../docs/desktop/assets/electron/swarms/swarm-run-watch-large.png";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Swarms — coordinate real agent sessions"
  description="Configure a swarm, review role assignments and approvals, then observe real runs, handoffs and artifacts in Agent Toolkit Operations."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Temporary roles · real sessions</p>
      <h1>Give a team one task. Keep every role in view.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Swarms coordinate reusable People through temporary responsibilities.
        Choose a recipe, review the topology and model choices, approve the
        plan, then follow what actually happens in Operations.
      </p>
      <figure class="pixel-frame page-hero-art">
        <img
          src={swarmStart.src}
          alt="Actual Agent Toolkit Swarm start review with recipe, role assignments, People bindings and model choices"
          fetchpriority="high"
        />
        <figcaption class="screen-caption">
          <span>REVIEW BEFORE START</span>
          <span>role · person · runner · model</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section section--deep">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <p class="section-kicker">A role is not a Person</p>
        <h2>Recipes define the responsibility. You choose who takes it.</h2>
        <p>
          Planner, implementer, reviewer, architect and QA remain ephemeral
          Swarm roles. Bind them to a compatible Person when you want a familiar
          collaborator, or choose Auto and let the canonical resolver create an
          ephemeral session.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="semantic-list">
        <div class="semantic-item">
          <span class="term">01 · SHAPE</span>
          <p>
            <strong>Inspect the recipe</strong>
            <br />
            Read the planned topology and its canonical role requirements.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="semantic-item">
          <span class="term">02 · BIND</span>
          <p>
            <strong>Choose the team</strong>
            <br />
            Override runner, model profile and optional Person per role.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="semantic-item">
          <span class="term">03 · REVIEW</span>
          <p>
            <strong>Check the gates</strong>
            <br />
            Review isolation, budgets, approval points and cleanup scope.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="semantic-item">
          <span class="term">04 · START</span>
          <p>
            <strong>Create actual sessions</strong>
            <br />
            The configured runner, backend and workspace determine runtime.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="semantic-item">
          <span class="term">05 · OBSERVE</span>
          <p>
            <strong>Follow the run</strong>
            <br />
            Inspect handoffs, artifacts, events and logs in Operations.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="semantic-item">
          <span class="term">06 · RECOVER</span>
          <p>
            <strong>Act on real state</strong>
            <br />
            Approve, pause, resume, stop or clean up with a review.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section section--glow">
    <div class="wrap scene-grid">
      <div class="scene-copy">
        <p class="section-kicker">
          Operations shows what. The World shows where.
        </p>
        <h2>Observe the run without turning the map into a graph.</h2>
        <p>
          Operations is the detailed view: it explains role state, human gates
          and recovery. The World provides a spatial overview of where live
          project work happens. A Person appears in a house only when backend
          evidence connects a real session to that project.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Approvals and human input surface in Attention</li>
          <li>Artifacts and handoffs stay attached to the run</li>
          <li>Completed work isn't falsely queued for review</li>
          <li>A headless record is never presented as a live agent</li>
        </ul>
        <a class="quiet-link" href="/docs/swarms/">
          Read the Swarm guide <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </div>
      <figure class="pixel-frame scene-image">
        <img
          src={swarmWatch.src}
          alt="Actual Agent Toolkit Swarm run inspector showing live state, roles and recoverable operations"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="screen-caption">
          <span>RUN INSPECTOR</span>
          <span>the exact run · actual state</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section section--deep">
    <div class="wrap closing-banner">
      <p class="section-kicker">No People required to begin</p>
      <h2>Start with Auto. Add your team over time.</h2>
      <p>
        Swarms remain useful even when your roster is empty. Bind durable People
        when those collaborators add real value.
      </p>
      <div class="button-row">
        <a class="button" href="/download/">
          Explore Desktop{" "}
          <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">
            →
          </span>
        </a>
        <a class="button secondary" href="/docs/swarm-roles/">
          Explore role taxonomy
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
